Bryan Walsh <simguy@cox.net> wrote:
> I've been playing around with the -std strict_ansi flag with cxx version 
> 6.5.  One thing that it does, besides changing the way the standard 
> header files   (iostream,cmath,etc) work, is that it also affects a 
> number of other header files in weird ways...
> Has anybody else experienced these difficulties? Does anybody have any 
> workarounds?

I ran into these when I worked in the HP Tru64 group.  One thing to
remember is that certain system functions or header files are only
supported under certain standards.  A later-edition POSIX function may
simply not be available for strict_ansi, which implies an earlier
standard.  One work-around could be to hack up your own versions of
the headers, removing the the standards-related #ifdef statements from
around the definitions and adding a "-I dir" flag to your command
line, where "dir" is the directory containing the modified headers.
Several iterations may be required to catch all the dependencies.

							- DDH



